--TEST--
A child that runs to its end without a top-level `return` hands its parent back `null`, while a `require` of that very same file is still `1` — ADR 0006 § *Decision*, against `rule:statements/a-require-expression-is-mixed`
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`: `spawn script` goes through a door that asks for
# `script.spawn` first, and deny-by-default means a case with no
# configuration cannot spawn at all. This grants it and nothing else.
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true
--FILE quiet.nvs--
<?nvs
// No top-level `return` anywhere in it, which is the whole fixture. It
// echoes so that both halves below can say the file actually ran, rather
// than pinning a value a file that never started would also have.
echo "the file ran\n";
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0006 § *Decision*: "A child that runs to its end without a top-level
// `return` hands back `null` — the value every Novis function without a
// `return` produces. This is deliberately not `require`'s `1`."
var $job = spawn script "quiet.nvs" with(output: "capture");
var $result = await $job;

// The child ended by running out of statements, not by failing: `ok` is the
// success row, so the `value` below is the answer and not the absence of one.
echo "ok=", $result->ok ? "true" : "false", "\n";
echo "output=", $result->output;
echo "value=", Core\Debug::render($result->value), "\n";

// The other half of the same decision, over the *same* file: what `rule:statements/a-require-expression-is-mixed`
// pins for the construct PHP has. Asserting only the child's `null`
// would still read plausibly if the `1` had simply moved.
var $required = require "quiet.nvs";
echo "required=", Core\Debug::render($required), "\n";
--EXPECT--
ok=true
output=the file ran
value=null
the file ran
required=int(1)
